00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:30.850
What is SAFe?

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:33.600
SAFe is Scale Agile Framework.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.169
So this was agile
for enterprises,

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:37.600
larger enterprises.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.789
And there's tons
of good information

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:41.289
out there on the internet.

00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:43.360
You can search upon it.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:46.630
Real quickly, the different
milestones or phases

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:48.610
that I've highlighted here.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:52.720
Project was a senior leadership
buy-in for the process.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.030
Yes, SAFe is what we
are going to adopt.

00:08:55.030 --> 00:09:00.510
Foundation is setting up of the
internal McKesson cloud team.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:02.590
The internal McKesson
cloud team is

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:05.470
responsible for the
connectivity to GCP

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:09.190
public cloud, all the cost
related conversations, all

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:11.680
the security related
conversations, new feature

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.620
enablement, giving access
to our development team

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.110
such as myself, they do it all.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:20.400
And the third phase
was migration.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.230
So migration could
mean different things

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.300
to various people.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:28.900
Specifically for my group,
we had some applications

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:32.610
which were running
at capacity, and we

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.450
thought these are good
candidates to be moved over

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.730
to public cloud from on prem.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:38.860
So that's what we did.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:41.470
We ended up migrating
those applications,

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:44.710
small, manageable
sizes, and the goal

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:49.000
is after 100% on
public cloud, we just

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:52.870
start building native
cloud applications,

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:56.060
reduce our technical debt,
and app modernization.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:58.480
Just keep our eyes open
for any opportunities

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.450
for modernization.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:04.350
As part of the
McKesson GCP adoption,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.990
the McKesson team got
access to a few free

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:11.530
on demand learning programs.

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.240
These were instrumental
for my team,

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.790
and they pretty much
acted like a slingshot.

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:20.520
Folks could go in
and at the self pace,

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.430
at their own leisure, learn
the new technology stack.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:25.960
And next thing you know,
they're building upon that.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:29.940
So I highly recommend
checking this feature out

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:31.980
with your Google account.

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:33.540
Executives.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:36.120
It's not just for the
hands on developers.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.070
There is some good
content out there

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:41.970
for leadership folks as well,
for transformation and just

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:43.470
cloud adoption.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:46.680
This one, I would call
it a replication strategy

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:50.210
as a foundational piece
and for going to cloud.

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:52.860
And why I'm calling
it foundational

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:57.140
is we decided to go with a
less complicated workflow

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:03.240
in the public cloud first and
not rule out our ERP day one.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.020
ERP systems are the ones which
are running our core business

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:08.310
of big package shipping.

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.540
So we do not want to go with
ERP day one into public cloud.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:13.870
Instead, we went with
analytical workloads

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:16.660
and the application, the
e-commerce workloads,

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.250
into public cloud.

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:21.580
And when you do that
with the original data,

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.830
it's going to initiate
on your ERP systems.

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:26.530
So how do you
compute on the cloud?

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:30.190
You cannot compute on the cloud
unless you get that enabled

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:31.940
source information.

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:35.290
And this is what the
context is for this slide.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:39.500
It's good to at
least think through,

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:41.817
how do you get your
ERP information,

00:11:41.817 --> 00:11:43.900
the orders which are getting
generated day in, day

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:46.870
out, how do you make them
available through one

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:50.510
single, structured
flow into cloud?

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:53.290
And from there, the
app development teams,

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:56.350
which are many, can
utilize the source data

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.390
and run with the
apps that they are

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:01.720
closest to in their own
subject matter expertise.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:04.420
And our VP would say--

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:08.710
he would often use the
phrase "10-yard teams"

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:11.980
who know how to run 10 yards,
but they would not know how

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.990
to score a touchdown.

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:16.540
You don't want to be in that
situation, where multiple app

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:19.480
development teams are just
using their own method,

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:23.170
opening up firewalls between
your on prem data centers

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:25.660
and onto your public cloud.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:29.020
And all of them
are more than often

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.000
replicating the
same information,

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:34.480
which can result into
very different problems,

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:37.060
like first is reduced
visibility for sure.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:39.430
You do not know what's
being moved from on prem

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:40.660
to the cloud.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:43.150
And then the other one
would be the security team

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:46.160
has limited
visibility into what--

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:48.730
is there patient information
that's flowing through?

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:52.600
And if yes, what are
the controls in place?

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:57.730
This one is a real graph
database screenshot

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:02.780
of just replications running
within the Oracle databases.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:03.790
This is DB Link.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.430
It does not cover the
other replications

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:09.170
that we are using outside
of that Oracle cluster.

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:11.590
This is just [INAUDIBLE]
Oracle cluster.

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:15.030
The good thing here is
this is all constraint,

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:16.490
and it's on prem.

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:20.020
But imagine if this was
the case for connectivity

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:22.960
from on prem to
the public cloud.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:25.200
You do not want that to happen.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.340
It should be nice, and
it should be measurable.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:30.630
It should be manageable.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.340
So replication, I
cannot emphasize enough.

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:36.800
It's definitely worth a pause
for the solution architects,

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:39.860
the enterprise architects,
and rethink it,

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:43.460
and try to get a grasp around,
no more uncontrolled port

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:46.100
holes to the cloud.

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:48.410
If there is an outage,
if the network is

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:51.297
running into contention,
what kind of insights

00:13:51.297 --> 00:13:52.130
do you have on this?

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:55.430
Is there a way to measure
who's running that workload?

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:57.110
Manageable outages.

00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:58.490
How do you manage outages?

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:01.230
How to think about and keep
a close eye on the network

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:02.210
bandwidth.

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:06.750
If app development team
A is using sales history,

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.800
or if they are moving sale
history to the public cloud,

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:12.550
unless you have a good
replication strategy,

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:15.620
the team B and C will never
know that the same information

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:17.990
that they are looking
for, it's already

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:20.810
on the public cloud,
versus them again

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:24.300
pulling the same information
through the wire.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:28.230
And again, when you think
about the replication tool,

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:31.320
think about a good [INAUDIBLE]
solution on the cloud

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:32.640
and use that.

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.920
You can pretty much
start creating that,

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:36.360
build on that story.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:39.160
From there, you can think about
the different heterogeneous

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:40.550
RDBMS solutions.

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:44.180
Perhaps also land those
events into Kafka Queue,

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:47.250
build APIs through it and so on.

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:51.690
In our case, we do have,
our application databases

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:57.600
are running on Oracle DB, and I
talked about the rich McKesson

00:14:57.600 --> 00:14:58.700
history.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:03.540
So for this specific version of
the one we have on the slide,

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:08.130
it's almost around 20 years
old, and more than often,

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.530
the implementation
reflects upon the skill

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:15.270
set that were on the team
at that point in time.

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.700
So the specific
application database

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:21.700
is running low latency calls
as app databases should,

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:23.730
but non-business
[INAUDIBLE],, it's also

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:27.480
extended to be doing
batch processing,

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:29.550
because that team
had Oracle talent.

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:32.850
So they ended up doing ETL
batch work non business hours,

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:35.110
and this is not uncommon.

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:39.060
So when we were thinking about
what's the ideal PaaS solution

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:40.800
to move away from
Oracle, because we

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:43.890
are running capacity, we
found the ideal candidate

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:45.840
to be MySQL.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:49.110
But again, we went
with PostGreSQL

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:53.380
for reasons I'll talk about
in the few next slides.

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:55.530
But good information
out there for folks

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:58.950
who are in the same boat,
multiple conversion utilities

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:02.010
available, like
migVISOR or Ora2pg.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:05.400
You also have GCP heterogeneous
tools product team.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.430
You can interview them and
see if they can do something

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:11.390
to ease that migration for you.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:17.650
So MySQL, I talked about, is
ideal candidate for app DBs.

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:19.810
It is thread based,
and it's better

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:21.310
for low latency connections.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:22.920
And it can support
many connections,

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:26.270
and that's what we see
happening with Oracle databases.

00:16:26.270 --> 00:16:30.240
And at the same time, you have
for a lot more success stories

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:34.670
of folks having moved
from Oracle to PostGreS.

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:36.280
So that's one of the--

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:41.050
this aspect of the many
success stories, this

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:43.630
weighed in heavily
for our decision.

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:48.280
The other specific-- the
utilization of sequences, we

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:53.890
[INAUDIBLE] sequences on Oracle,
we used of triggers heavily,

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:56.680
which are also
supported on PostGreSQL.

00:16:56.680 --> 00:17:01.330
And we are heavy into
materialized views.

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:02.560
Do not judge.

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:05.890
This is just how it exists,
just in the center here.

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:08.859
And we do use
Oracle partitioning.

00:17:08.859 --> 00:17:13.420
Our success criteria was
also, we do not have--

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:18.290
get it done with what do
you have, so more with less.

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:21.970
The goal was to make minimal
changes to the Java app

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:26.440
code and minimum changes to the
current legacy application--

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:28.390
I would call legacy now--

00:17:28.390 --> 00:17:30.790
and still move away from Oracle.

00:17:30.790 --> 00:17:35.530
Given those guardrails,
PostGreS is our best candidate

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:37.210
that we ended up picking.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:40.960
Been happy in an Elastic
shop for almost five years,

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:42.220
we started on prem.

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.680
We were running
capacity, so we ended up

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:49.160
moving our Elastic cache
databases to Azure.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:52.780
Azure, at the time, did not
have PaaS service, which

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:55.210
could support aggregate
functions, which we heavily

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:57.620
utilize, "group bys" and so on.

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:00.730
So we had to fall back
into the IES setup.

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.980
Now, IES, natively,
for folks who

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:05.810
have built infrastructure
as a service,

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:08.700
it's not a walk in the park.

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:10.090
It's not an easy thing to do.

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:13.090
And what further complicates
is the bigger these cache

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:17.180
databases become, the more
are the failure points,

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:19.510
the higher is the risk matrix.

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:21.910
Currently, we are
facing, on an IES stack,

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:24.580
the bigger disks which are
attached to these cache

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:28.150
databases need encryption,
and these encryption processes

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:30.460
may run for two, three days.

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.420
That's our biggest
failure point.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.050
That might hit our SLAs.

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:36.250
So we had been meaning
to get out of IES,

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:40.690
and very recently, as of three
months back, Elastic Cloud,

00:18:40.690 --> 00:18:43.830
they announced a [INAUDIBLE]
for HIPAA compliance.

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:45.070
That's excellent.

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:48.950
We need HIPAA for all the
workloads we put on the cloud,

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:51.620
and there's also aggregate
function support.

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:52.930
Where are we on this journey?

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:56.290
We did check out our
application compatibility

00:18:56.290 --> 00:18:58.930
on the Elastic Cloud,
Elastic Service.

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:02.140
And we're also looking
into the GCP credits

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:05.620
that we can flow through
the Elastic Cloud instance.

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:07.520
This was an interesting concept.

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:14.320
If you have an Oracle
database, and the ask

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:17.300
is to move away from
Oracle database,

00:19:17.300 --> 00:19:21.850
the first thought goes into
finding another database

00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:22.960
on the cloud.

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:24.380
So we were no different.

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:27.640
We have an information warehouse
just like many other folks do.

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:29.980
Actually, every shop as
an information warehouse.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:34.630
So if you reflect upon what was
the reason why that information

00:19:34.630 --> 00:19:37.060
warehouse came into
existence, we pretty much

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:40.000
reflected upon
ourselves, and it had

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.840
to be a fact-based
quest, because I

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:44.850
report into data and analytics.

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:48.070
So the data has to back
it up for any reasoning.

00:19:48.070 --> 00:19:49.990
And we trended each
and every pattern,

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:52.730
what are the workloads running
in the information warehouse?

00:19:52.730 --> 00:19:55.690
We came to the conclusion
that there were only

00:19:55.690 --> 00:20:00.850
a handful of data analysts
who were using that database

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:04.300
to run queries, and
these are the guys

00:20:04.300 --> 00:20:06.620
who were connecting through
Access DB and so on.

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:08.037
So essentially,
what they're doing

00:20:08.037 --> 00:20:10.248
is just pulling information
out, and if that's

00:20:10.248 --> 00:20:12.040
what they're doing and
not running queries,

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:13.850
why do you need a database?

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:17.320
So when we thought about that
concept, one of our vendors,

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:20.680
Impetus, they have a utility
called Automated Workload

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:22.180
Transformation.

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:23.620
What does the tool do?

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:27.700
You put in different flavors of
software on the left hand side,

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:30.200
and automagically,
on the other side,

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:32.710
a different version comes out.

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:35.500
In our case, we had
PLSQL procedures,

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:40.150
we had C++ applications built
over the last 20 plus years,

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:43.150
and we had OWB, Oracle
Warehouse Builder,

00:20:43.150 --> 00:20:46.050
which is also end of
life, Oracle EDL2.

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:50.230
So we push all that application
on the left hand side as input

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:54.110
to the Workload Transformation,
and we [INAUDIBLE] open source

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:56.320
as our model and PaaS.

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:57.970
We wanted it to
be converted into

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:00.010
a Dataproc friendly version.

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:03.460
In our case, we converted
that to [? Deviceproc, ?]

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:06.010
and on that little
black screen, very

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:09.550
hard to interpret, this as
our 14,000 lines of code.

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:12.730
The way the Workload
Transformation works is it

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:15.220
comments the original
application code,

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:19.800
and right underneath, it's going
to put the converted block.

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:22.820
So it's easy to diagnose.

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:26.500
The takeaway would be, just
because you have a database

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:29.350
application, it doesn't
have to be solved

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:31.090
from database to database.

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:35.960
There could be other ways
to solve it without a DB.

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:41.600
We have had different Hadoop
flavors over the years.

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:45.910
I started out with Hortonworks,
before Hortonworks and Cloudera

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:46.910
combined.

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:52.670
But more recently, we shut down
our on prem Cloudera cluster

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:56.480
and removed our workloads
to convert to Dataproc.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:59.510
And just going into the
weeds here a little bit,

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:01.910
we did work with our internal
cloud team and our network

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:06.060
teams to open up [INAUDIBLE],,
utilized on this [? CP ?]

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:08.570
utility within Hadoop
framework to move data

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:12.650
from HDFS into the
buckets with GCS Buckets.

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:14.870
The nice thing
about Google Cloud

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:18.110
is, by default,
storage is encrypted,

00:22:18.110 --> 00:22:20.120
so you cut down that
additional layer.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:23.510
Saves a lot of time not
having to encrypt again.

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:28.280
The assets that we use,
again, are HIPAA compliant.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.850
For Metastore, we do use
MySQL, a small instance,

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:34.730
and it's dual
replicated for folks

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:37.780
who do not know why Metastore
has been pulled out.

00:22:37.780 --> 00:22:42.020
The clusters, as such, are
where this compute is expensive

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:44.575
and storage is cheap
on public cloud.

00:22:44.575 --> 00:22:45.950
So the goal should
be to minimize

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:47.510
the utilization of compute.

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:52.050
If you're not using it,
then why is it running 24/7?

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:55.653
That's called transient
cluster, that we destroy it

00:22:55.653 --> 00:22:56.820
when they're not being used.

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:58.650
So our Dataproc
clusters that come

00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:03.270
into being for one or two
hours, we run the workload,

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:04.880
and then they vanish.

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:06.810
And we do the same
for table structures

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:07.950
and all that good stuff.

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:09.270
It's in the Metastore.

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:11.190
That's what it's doing.

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:13.590
Transiency is super important.

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:15.000
Keep an eye on the cost.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.550
I've been doing cloud
for almost five years

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:19.320
here, so I can
tell the difference

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:21.600
between the old legacy
[? thing ?] of cutting

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:25.470
a big check, and after,
towards the end of five

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:30.227
years lifecycle, your
SLAs start taking a hit.

00:23:30.227 --> 00:23:31.560
There's only so much you can do.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:34.260
You never lose cost because
your hardware can only

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:35.130
support that much.

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:38.820
Your apps might take a hit,
but you're not spending extra.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:41.640
Whereas on the
cloud, very quickly,

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:43.890
as your workload and
your data increases,

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:45.860
you might be paying
through the nose.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:49.740
It's imperative, keep your
eyes on the cost optimized.

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:52.770
We ordered our cost
[? chart ?] on a monthly basis,

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:56.040
and also shared our insights
with our senior leadership

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.530
on how much are we spending
and what applications

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:00.380
are chewing up that cost.

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:02.130
LOKESH KRIS: That's
great to hear, Anshul.

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:04.170
As you can see,
McKesson addressed

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:07.890
several aspirational goals here.

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:10.060
They wanted to move
into open source option,

00:24:10.060 --> 00:24:12.780
and that they wanted to
modernize their analytics,

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:16.790
implement visualization stack,
and more importantly, modernize

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:20.430
a skill set of the organization
and adapt and adopt trends.

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:23.580
Enterprises increasingly
want to adopt open source

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:26.940
and move away from licensed
software wherever possible.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:28.920
As a business grows,
and more and more data

00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:31.830
is made available, and the
need to modernize analytics

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:34.680
[INAUDIBLE] becomes
increasingly important.

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:37.710
Translating this information
into insightful visualizations

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:39.660
becomes a necessity.

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:42.660
Enterprises want to adapt
to meet these modernization

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:45.000
challenges, and it
includes modernizing

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.100
the skill set as well.

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:48.960
As enterprises evolve
after adoption,

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:53.670
the focus is on lean DevOps and
control their ongoing spend.

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:56.620
And that was a
wonderful story, Anshul,

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:59.760
and thank you for walking
us through your highly

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:02.970
informative and educative
data modernization journey.

00:25:02.970 --> 00:25:04.890
It is especially
interesting to see

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:08.203
how you started this effort
off to integrate siloed

00:25:08.203 --> 00:25:09.870
and [INAUDIBLE] within
your organization

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:11.490
with safe methodology,
and address

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:15.650
some very complex challenges
on your replication

00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:18.100
and application
databases, your cache

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:21.120
databases, your information
warehouse, and also

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:24.385
the hardware end of life.

00:25:24.385 --> 00:25:26.760
These are some of the challenges
that all the enterprises

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:27.840
face today.

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:31.280
Thank you, once again,
and thank you very much.

